 ("h2", "3.1 Who Were the Marathas?"),
 ("p", "The Marathas are a people native to the Deccan plateau, mainly present-day Maharashtra, identified by the Marathi language, which has had a rich and continuous literary history since the 12th century. In the 13th century most of Maharashtra was ruled by the **Yadava dynasty** with Devagiri (present-day Daulatabad) as capital; in the early 14th century the Khilji Sultanate overcame them."),
 ("p", "Through these political changes the **bhakti** tradition (devotion to the divine) continued. Between the 7th and 17th centuries saints from diverse sections of society composed devotional songs in the languages of ordinary people. In Maharashtra, Dnyaneshwar, Namdev, Tukaram and Ramdas translated texts like the Upanishads and the Gita into Marathi and some also worked on social organisation and political awareness. This gave society a strong cultural foundation for later political unity."),
 ("h2", "3.2 Chhatrapati Shivaji: Founding of Maratha Power"),
